, but it seems to me, that - while being intuitive - subclassing `GitRepo` for `AnnexRepo` isn't particularly useful.\n\nI'm not clear whether start transitioning to core that way is helping that process or making it harder.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1578333469,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MTI0MTc4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3988#issuecomment-571241784"},"message":"a minor comment on one aspect:\n\n\u003e * establish new base classes for `GitRepo`, `AnnexRepo`, `Dataset`, each with the name prefix `Core`, and place them, and only these new classes under `datalad/core`. The original classes will then derive from the new bases while keeping there API 100% intact.\n\nI would probably recommend to avoid `Core` prefix.  Since not all classes of the `.core` would have `Core` prefix, better to not introduce such only to some but not the other.  Modular Python structure already allows for unambiguous imports (from unambigous `.core` vs `.support`), and it would only be a question of which ones are exposed as part of the `datalad.api` (and keeping a mental note to keep an eye on imports while coding).  For `.api`, we could keep it `.support` ones to start with, and switch later on to the `.core` without requiring any code tune up.  Also it would make it easier for downstream projects to not require adjusting code (`GitRepo` vs `CoreGitRepo`), and only `import` statements.","files":null}]}